From 7626fcb943432bb4bff8498a6b31fd28676850fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 05:55:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/20] feat(reconciler): deploy a failed service again when
 its spec changes

The R3-1 API accepts updates of a Failed service and bumps its
generation, but the state machine only let Failed move to Terminating,
so the update was stored and never acted on: the only way out of
Failed was to delete and re-create the service.

Failed gains one transition, Failed -> Pending. A Failed service whose
generation is ahead of its observedGeneration moves to Pending in a
pass without side effects, and is then deployed like a new service:
Pending -> Scheduling evaluates the policy, the held allocation is
reused when it still fits and replaced otherwise, and an instance that
compensation could not stop is stopped before the new deployment run
starts. Pending rather than Scheduling was picked so that redeploying
a failed service never skips the policy check or any later step.

There is still no automatic retry: a Failed service whose spec did not
change is left alone.
